  "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 6\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[20, 23, 13], [9, 12, 15], [30, 26, 20], [21, 28, 16], [25, 7, 29], [12, 23, 20]]\n    machine_assignments = [[0, 2, 1], [2, 1, 0], [2, 1, 0], [1, 0, 2], [0, 2, 1], [2, 1, 0]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
  "LLM_description": "To: Production Leadership Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated Scheduling for Current Job Shop Workload\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized machines (workstations) processing a set of customer orders. Each job follows a defined routing with a fixed sequence of operations. Every operation must run on a specific machine for a known duration, and processing times vary by operation and workstation. The mix of routings and durations creates competition for shared machines and introduces the risk of bottlenecks if sequencing is not carefully managed.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe need to build a feasible, shop-wide production schedule that sequences all operations across all machines while honoring each job\u2019s prescribed order of work. Because multiple jobs require access to the same machines at different points in their routings, the timing of each operation affects downstream availability and can either congest or starve subsequent work centers. Poorly coordinated start times can lead to extended queues, idle assets, and missed delivery windows. A disciplined schedule will balance machine loading, keep work flowing, and finish the full workload as early as possible.\n\nOperating Rules and Constraints\n- Operation order within each job is fixed. The next operation cannot begin until the prior one for that job has finished.\n- Each operation is assigned to a specific machine and cannot be processed elsewhere.\n- Each machine can process a single operation at a time. No overlapping work is permitted.\n- No preemption is allowed. Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption.\n- Waiting between operations is possible if the next required machine is occupied; the schedule should minimize avoidable waiting and machine idle time while respecting the rules above.\n\nGoal\nCreate a start\u2013finish schedule for all operations that completes the entire set of jobs in the shortest possible total time. In practical terms, success is measured by the earliest finish of the last job (overall makespan), achieved without violating machine capacity or job sequencing requirements. Clear dispatch lists and start times for each machine will guide execution and help stabilize flow across the shop.\n\nRationale\nGiven the variety of routings and processing times, this is a tightly coupled coordination problem. Early sequencing decisions on one machine influence the availability of work on others, and small delays can ripple throughout the schedule. By laying out a coherent plan that respects machine exclusivity and job order, we will reduce bottlenecks, better utilize constrained resources, and protect on-time delivery performance.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 6\n- Number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Job-by-job production schedule (operations must be performed in the listed sequence):\n  Job 1: Op1 on Machine 0 for 20 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 23 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 13 time units\n  Job 2: Op1 on Machine 2 for 9 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 12 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 15 time units\n  Job 6: Op1 on Machine 2 for 12 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 23 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 20 time units\n  Job 5: Op1 on Machine 0 for 25 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 7 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 29 time units\n  Job 4: Op1 on Machine 1 for 21 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 28 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 16 time units\n  Job 3: Op1 on Machine 2 for 30 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 26 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 20 time units",
